  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A return to a busier, stormier pattern today, \nthrough the weekend. \n\nUpper ridging begins to flatten today in response to a weak \ntrough and associated surface front moving south from the Great \nLakes region. Westerly flow increases today ahead of the front\nand may limit convection somewhat, especially east of the \nmountains. Coverage is not expected to be widespread, but\ngreatest chance will be for the northern half of the forecast\narea. This front will eventually slow and stall across the\nCarolinas on Friday and may eventually move back north as a warm\nfront over the weekend. This will keep a greater chance for rain\nand storms beginning Friday, persisting through the weekend and\ninto early next week. \n\n\nWe are included in a Marginal Risk for excessive rainfall from \nWPC over the weekend given the slow-moving/stalling front along \nwith high PWATs, so isolated flash flooding will be possible. \nShear for storm organization looks weak today given the front \nstill to the north. Isolated damaging winds will be possible \nwhere/if storms can briefly take advantage and become severe. \nShear and instability both improve Friday and Saturday, and \nthese look like better chances for damaging winds and also flash\nflooding, especially from the Blue Ridge westward. \n\nThe relative humidity will also increase Friday through the\nweekend, making it muggier. For Monday into Tuesday, we look \nmostly dry, with a few showers/isolated storms possible for the \nsouthern Blue Ridge. The next frontal passage will occur the \nfollowing Wednesday.",
